  "client_view_text": " Here's a statement from Wikipedia that I'd like to use as a starting point:\n\nA write-ahead log is a record of all database operations, including updates that have not yet been written to the database, that are written in an orderly fashion to a separate, permanent media such as disk, tape, or flash storage.\n\nA key aspect of a write-ahead log is that it is maintained independently of the database itself. If a database crashes, an external process can then take a snapshot of the current database and bring it up to date with the transactions recorded in the log, thus recovering from the crash.\n\nHere are some additional points that I'd Write a log record in the format required to write it to the disk by a write-ahead log.\nWhen a database crashes, a database can recover using the data written to the disk. A write-ahead log allows a database to recover after a crash by allowing it to replay the transactions that were being written to the disk at the time of the crash. In a write-ahead log, every operation that changes the database state is written to the log before the actual data is written to the disk. This ensures that if the database crashes before the operation has been written to the disk, the log will contain enough information for the database to recover.\nEach log record in a write-ahead log contains the following information: the transaction ID (TID), the operation type (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, or REDO), the primary key of the affected table, the old value of the record before the operation, the new value of the record after the operation, and a timestamp of when the operation was written to the log.\nA log record is written to the disk at a time T (say, 23:00:00) and has the following information: TID = 123, operation = UPDATE, table = EMPLOYEE, primary key = EMP001, old value = {name"
}